In the project at `/tmp/sv-b08824fc9b1c49d89e915bdc3d02e35d/before/`, there are many files with this pattern:

```csharp
JsonSerializer.Serialize(writer, document.RootElement);
```

This needs to be replaced with the AOT-safe equivalent:
```csharp
document.RootElement.WriteTo(writer);
```

The files are all `.Serialization.cs` files in the project. The pattern occurs in TWO contexts:

1. **Inside `#else` blocks** (after `#if NET6_0_OR_GREATER` / `writer.WriteRawValue(...)`) - These won't compile for net9.0 anyway, but fix them for correctness.

2. **In normal code** (the "format W" fallback for additional properties) - Pattern looks like:
```csharp
using (JsonDocument document = JsonDocument.Parse(property.Value.GetRawText(), ModelSerializationExtensions.JsonDocumentOptions))
{
    JsonSerializer.Serialize(writer, document.RootElement);
}
```
Replace with:
```csharp
using (JsonDocument document = JsonDocument.Parse(property.Value.GetRawText(), ModelSerializationExtensions.JsonDocumentOptions))
{
    document.RootElement.WriteTo(writer);
}
```

Or sometimes it's:
```csharp
using (JsonDocument document = JsonDocument.Parse(Properties, ModelSerializationExtensions.JsonDocumentOptions))
{
    JsonSerializer.Serialize(writer, document.RootElement);
}
```
Replace with:
```csharp
using (JsonDocument document = JsonDocument.Parse(Properties, ModelSerializationExtensions.JsonDocumentOptions))
{
    document.RootElement.WriteTo(writer);
}
```

Run this command to find all files:
```bash
cd /tmp/sv-b08824fc9b1c49d89e915bdc3d02e35d/before && grep -rln 'JsonSerializer.Serialize(writer, document.RootElement)' --include='*.cs' | grep -v '/bin/' | grep -v '/obj/'
```

Then for each file, replace ALL occurrences of `JsonSerializer.Serialize(writer, document.RootElement)` with `document.RootElement.WriteTo(writer)`. Use sed:
```bash
cd /tmp/sv-b08824fc9b1c49d89e915bdc3d02e35d/before && find . -name '*.cs' -not -path '*/bin/*' -not -path '*/obj/*' -exec grep -l 'JsonSerializer.Serialize(writer, document.RootElement)' {} \; | xargs sed -i 's/JsonSerializer.Serialize(writer, document.RootElement)/document.RootElement.WriteTo(writer)/g'
```

After doing the replacement, verify with:
```bash
grep -rn 'JsonSerializer.Serialize(writer, document.RootElement)' --include='*.cs' | grep -v '/bin/' | grep -v '/obj/'
```
This should return no results.

Also check if there are any files that have `using System.Text.Json;` that are now unused - but DON'T remove them since they likely use JsonElement or JsonDocument.

